          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Benazir Bhutto</strong><br>\nBenazir Bhutto was born on June 21, 1953, in Karachi, Pakistan, into a prominent political family. Her father, Zulfikar Ali Bhutto, was Pakistan's President and later Prime Minister, and her mother, Nusrat Bhutto, was also a politician. Benazir grew up in a politically charged atmosphere, receiving her early education in Pakistan. She later went to Harvard University and the University of Oxford, where she honed her political ideas and leadership skills.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Ascent</strong><br>\nAfter completing her education, Benazir Bhutto returned to Pakistan in 1977, the same year her father's government was overthrown by a military coup. She inherited the leadership of the Pakistan People's Party (PPP) after her father's execution in 1979. Benazir's political life was marked by her opposition to military rule, and she was detained multiple times for her political activities.</p>\n\n<p><strong>First Term as Prime Minister</strong><br>\nIn 1988, Benazir Bhutto made history by becoming the first woman to head the government of a Muslim-majority country. During her first term as Prime Minister, she sought to implement social reforms and to lift the poor's economic status. However, her tenure was challenged by issues of corruption, governance difficulties, and power struggles, leading to her dismissal by the then-president in 1990.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
