            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Political Exile</strong><br>\nFollowing her dismissal, Benazir Bhutto spent several years in exile, primarily in London and Dubai. During this time, she continued to be an active member of the PPP and a vocal opponent of the government's policies. Her time abroad was spent organizing party work and building international support for her return to power in Pakistan.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Second Term in Office</strong><br>\nBenazir Bhutto was re-elected as Prime Minister in 1993. Her second term focused on improving Pakistan's economy and foreign policy. She also faced numerous challenges, including increased ethnic and sectarian violence in Pakistan, and her government was again dismissed in 1996 amidst scandal and charges of mismanagement and corruption.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Political Challenges</strong><br>\nAfter being dismissed from her second term, Benazir Bhutto again faced legal challenges and accusations. Despite these setbacks, she remained a significant figure in Pakistani politics. Her later years were spent rallying for democracy and challenging the military's involvement in politics, which had become a defining feature of Pakistani governance.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
